{"labs": [{"id": 0, "type": "scholar", "stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "id": 0, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "_data": []}, {"type": "audio", "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"coverimg": "engenn6le15ob05re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "engenn6le15ob05re2aud01", "images": [{"image": "engenn6le15ob05re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "", "time": "0"}, {"image": "engenn6le15ob05re2img01.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "Antarctica has always been a land of unexplored mysteries. Its size and shape were unknown until Captain James Cook sailed around the continent in 1770. Early maps of Antarctica showed only a portion  and contained many mistakes. Since then, satellite pictures have helped us build an accurate map of the whole continent.", "time": "100"}, {"image": "engenn6le15ob05re2img02.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "Antarctica was initially known as the land of commercial opportunity. People traveled to Antarctica to hunt seals or whales. Over the years, scientific research replaced hunting wildlife. Antarctica has now become a continent of science and research.", "time": "21050"}, {"image": "engenn6le15ob05re2img03.jpg", "id": 3, "text": "Eighteen countries have built scientific research stations that operate all year long. Studies range from finding out more about the ozone layer to biological studies on endangered wildlife.\nPeople do not hunt animals in Antarctica anymore. Instead, they would rather find ways to improve the world. Scientists would rather use the continent for peaceful activities. This is why a treaty was signed in 1959 to protect Antarctica.", "time": "36550"}]}}], "title": "", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "_data": []}, {"type": "text", "contents": [{"type": "audio", "value": "engenn6le15ob05re5aud01"}], "title": "", "template_override": 1, "id": 2, "_data": []}, {"type": "audio", "contents": [], "title": "", "template_override": 1, "id": 3, "_data": []}], "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"t